# Built-in Tools

Always available (call directly, already in scope):

- `editFile(path: string, oldString: string, newString: string, opts?: { replaceAll?: boolean }): { ok: boolean; replacements: number; error?: string }` — Replace an exact string in a file. Fails if oldString is missing or non-unique (unless replaceAll).
- `forget(key: string): { ok: boolean; error?: string }` — Delete a remembered fact by key.
- `glob(pattern: string, opts?: { cwd?: string }): { ok: boolean; paths: string[]; error?: string }` — Find files matching a glob pattern (supports ** and *). Skips node_modules and .git.
- `grep(pattern: string, opts?: { path?: string; glob?: string; ignoreCase?: boolean; maxMatches?: number }): { ok: boolean; matches: { file: string; line: number; text: string }[]; truncated: boolean; error?: string }` — Search file contents for a pattern. Uses ripgrep, falling back to grep -rn. Returns file/line/text matches.
- `listDir(path: string): { ok: boolean; entries: string[]; error?: string }` — List the entries (files and directories) in a directory.
- `readFile(path: string, opts?: { offset?: number; limit?: number }): { ok: boolean; content: string; raw: string; lines: number; truncated: boolean; error?: string }` — Read a text file. `content` has 1-based line numbers (useful for referencing lines in editFile);
`raw` has the unmodified text (use this for JSON.parse and structured data).
- `recall(key: string): { ok: boolean; value: unknown; found: boolean }` — Recall a previously remembered fact by key. found=false if it was never stored.
- `recallAll(): { ok: boolean; facts: Record<string, unknown> }` — Return all remembered facts as a key to value object.
- `remember(key: string, value: unknown): { ok: boolean; error?: string }` — Persist a fact across sessions under a key (durable, space-scoped).
- `todoRead(): {
  ok: boolean;
  items: Array<{ content: string; status: string }>;
}` — Read the current task checklist.
- `todoWrite(items: Array<{ content: string; status: 'pending' | 'in_progress' | 'completed' }>): { ok: boolean; count: number }` — Set the current task checklist (replaces the whole list), render it, and persist it.
- `webFetch(url: string, opts?: { maxChars?: number; format?: 'text' | 'html' | 'markdown'; render?: 'auto' | 'force' | 'off' }): Promise<{ ok: boolean; status: number; content: string; truncated: boolean; rendered?: boolean; error?: string }>` — Fetch a URL and return its body. By default the HTML is reduced to readable text
(tags stripped, entities decoded); pass `{ format: 'markdown' }` to keep structure
(headings, links, lists, bold/italic, code) as Markdown instead of flattening it —
better for research synthesis, where "what links to what" and section structure
matter; pass `{ format: 'html' }` for the raw source.

A plain `fetch` returns only server-sent HTML, so a **client-rendered (SPA / JS-heavy)**
page comes back as an empty shell with no usable content. `render` controls the fallback to
the in-cluster headless-browser render service (the same one `webSearch` uses), which
executes JavaScript and returns the rendered DOM: `'auto'` (default) re-fetches through the
render service only when the plain fetch looks dynamic (thin content + an app-shell/JS-gated
page) or was bot-walled (403/429), and keeps whichever yields more readable text; `'force'`
always renders; `'off'` never does (plain fetch only). The fallback is a no-op when
RENDER_SERVICE_URL is unset (e.g. local dev). `rendered` reports whether the returned content
came from the render service.
- `type WebSearchResult = {
  ok: boolean;
  query: string;
  answer: string;
  results: Array<{ title: string; url: string; snippet: string; score: number }>;
  error?: string;
};
declare async function webSearch(query: string, opts?: {
    depth?: 'basic' | 'advanced';
    maxResults?: number;
    topic?: 'general' | 'news';
    timeRange?: 'day' | 'week' | 'month' | 'year';
    provider?: 'tavily' | 'bing' | 'duckduckgo' | 'auto';
  }): Promise<{
  ok: boolean;
  query: string;
  answer: string;
  results: Array<{ title: string; url: string; snippet: string; score: number }>;
  error?: string;
}>` — Search the web. Returns ranked results and (Tavily only) an AI answer. `provider`
(default `'auto'`): `'tavily'` requires TAVILY_API_KEY; `'bing'` renders Bing's
results page with JavaScript via the in-cluster headless-browser service
(RENDER_SERVICE_URL) and scrapes it — no key, real rendered DOM, no `answer`;
`'duckduckgo'` scrapes the no-JS HTML endpoint (no key, lower-quality ranking, no
`answer`); `'auto'` tries the best available in order — Tavily (when keyed) → Bing
(when RENDER_SERVICE_URL is set) → DuckDuckGo — returning the first with results.
(Bing rather than Google: Google serves datacenter IPs a consent/bot redirect loop that
never renders, whereas Bing renders cleanly.) `topic: 'news'` + `timeRange` bias results
toward recent coverage instead of evergreen pages (Tavily only) — use for "latest
developments" angles instead of faking recency in the query text.
- `writeFile(path: string, content: string): { ok: boolean; bytes: number; error?: string }` — Create or overwrite a file with exact content (binary-safe, no shell quoting).

